---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import MoonGlobe from '../../components/MoonGlobe.astro';
import Heading from '../../components/Heading.astro';
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

let celestial: any = null;
try {
  celestial = JSON.parse(
    fs.readFileSync(path.resolve('public/data/moon.json'), 'utf8')
  );
} catch { /* rendered without live values */ }

const m = celestial?.moon ?? {};

const fmtStamp = (s: string | null | undefined) => {
  if (!s) return '—';
  try {
    return new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', {
      dateStyle: 'medium', timeStyle: 'short', timeZone: 'UTC',
    }).format(new Date(s + ':00:00Z')) + ' UTC';
  } catch { return s; }
};

const phaseLabel = (m.phase_name ?? '').replace(/_/g, ' ');
const km = (v: number) => (v == null ? '—' : Math.round(v).toLocaleString('en-US'));

// The lunar results from the study, if it has been run: eight phase buckets
// against three return horizons.
let study: any = null;
try {
  study = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.resolve('public/data/study.json'), 'utf8'));
} catch { /* the test section says it has not run */ }
const HORIZON: Record<string, string> = { IDX_daily: 'Same day', IDX_fwd_5d: 'Next 5 days', IDX_fwd_21d: 'Next 21 days' };
const PHASES = ['new', 'waxing_crescent', 'first_quarter', 'waxing_gibbous', 'full', 'waning_gibbous', 'last_quarter', 'waning_crescent'];
const lunar = (study?.results ?? [])
  .filter((r: any) => r.hypothesis === 'lunar_phase')
  .sort((a: any, b: any) =>
    Object.keys(HORIZON).indexOf(a.series) - Object.keys(HORIZON).indexOf(b.series) ||
    PHASES.indexOf(a.bucket) - PHASES.indexOf(b.bucket));
const lunarRawSig = lunar.filter((r: any) => r.p_value < 0.05).length;
const lunarBh = lunar.filter((r: any) => (r.p_bh ?? 1) < 0.10).length;
const lunarBonf = lunar.filter((r: any) => (r.p_bonferroni ?? 1) < 0.05).length;
const bp = (v: number | null | undefined) =>
  v == null || !Number.isFinite(v) ? '—' : `${v < 0 ? '−' : ''}${Math.abs(v * 10000).toFixed(1)}`;
const pv = (v: number | null | undefined) =>
  v == null ? '—' : v < 0.0001 ? '<0.0001' : v.toFixed(4);

const sections = [
  { href: '/studies/', label: 'All studies' },
  { id: 'state', label: 'The Moon today' },
  { id: 'scrubbing', label: 'Scrubbing back' },
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why this page exists' },
  { id: 'test', label: 'The test' },
];
---

<Base
  title="The Moon"
  description="The Moon rendered from real ephemeris geometry, and the pre-registered test of lunar phase against equity returns."
  sections={sections}
>
  <div class="page">
    <Heading level={1}>The Moon</Heading>
    <p>
      Rendered from JPL DE440s. The terminator is not drawn: the light sits
      along the true Moon to Sun vector and the camera along the true Moon to
      Earth vector, so the phase is a consequence of the geometry rather than a
      parameter. Libration is applied as a real rotation, which is why the limb
      detail shifts over a month. The surface is NASA LROC imagery over the
      LOLA elevation model.
    </p>

    <div class="full block dark moon-block">
      <MoonGlobe height="62vh" />
    </div>

    <Heading id="state">The Moon today</Heading>
    <section class="readout" aria-label="Lunar state for the selected date">
      <div><dt>Phase</dt><dd data-moon="phase">{phaseLabel || '—'}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Illuminated</dt><dd class="num" data-moon="illuminated">{m.illuminated != null ? (m.illuminated * 100).toFixed(1) + '%' : '—'}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Phase angle</dt><dd class="num" data-moon="phase_angle">{m.phase_angle != null ? m.phase_angle.toFixed(1) + '°' : '—'}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Distance</dt><dd class="num" data-moon="distance">{km(m.distance_km)} km</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Libration, longitude</dt><dd class="num" data-moon="lib_lon">{m.libration_longitude != null ? (m.libration_longitude > 0 ? '+' : '') + m.libration_longitude.toFixed(2) + '°' : '—'}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Libration, latitude</dt><dd class="num" data-moon="lib_lat">{m.libration_latitude != null ? (m.libration_latitude > 0 ? '+' : '') + m.libration_latitude.toFixed(2) + '°' : '—'}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Next new moon</dt><dd class="num stamp">{fmtStamp(m.next_new_moon)}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Next full moon</dt><dd class="num stamp">{fmtStamp(m.next_full_moon)}</dd></div>
    </section>

    <p class="note">
      The six figures above follow the date slider. The next new and full moon
      timestamps do not, they are always the next ones from today, since a
      &ldquo;next full moon&rdquo; relative to a date two years ago is a fact
      about the past dressed up as a forecast.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      Distance sits at the {m.distance_percentile != null ? (m.distance_percentile * 100).toFixed(0) : '—'}th
      percentile of the perigee–apogee range (356,500–406,700 km). The next new
      and full moons are found by searching for the elongation crossing, not by
      adding 29.53 days to the last one, that accumulates error.
    </p>

    <Heading id="scrubbing">Scrubbing back</Heading>
    <p>
      The slider under the render moves through four years of daily geometry,
      two back and two forward. Every frame is an ephemeris lookup rather than
      an interpolation, so a date last spring shows the Moon as it actually
      was, including the libration that had rolled a different stretch of the
      limb into view. Run a month to watch a full lunation, and the wobble is
      obvious: the face is not fixed.
    </p>

    <Heading id="why">Why this page exists</Heading>
    <p>
      Lunar phase is a real, published hypothesis. Yuan and Zheng's
      <em>Are Investors Moonstruck?</em> reported lower equity returns around the
      full moon across a large sample of countries, and it is a paper this study
      replicates against rather than dismisses. It sits in Tier 2 of the
      hypothesis list: cyclical, well-documented, and with no mechanism anyone
      has been able to state.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rendering above is careful for the same reason the statistics below
      are. A page that got the astronomy wrong while claiming to test it
      rigorously would be making the opposite point to the intended one.
    </p>

    <Heading id="test">The test</Heading>
    {lunar.length ? (
      <>
        <p>
          Returns on the {study.series.label}, <span class="nowrap">{study.series.start}</span> to
          {' '}<span class="nowrap">{study.series.end}</span>, bucketed by the eight lunar phases and compared
          with every other day, at three horizons. Each row is one test of the
          {' '}{study.n_tests} the study ran, and every p-value is corrected
          across all of them, not only across these.
        </p>
        <dl class="figures">
          <div><dt>Lunar tests</dt><dd>{lunar.length}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>p &lt; 0.05, uncorrected</dt><dd>{lunarRawSig}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Survive BH, q &lt; 0.10</dt><dd>{lunarBh}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Survive Bonferroni</dt><dd>{lunarBonf}</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <div class="full table-scroll">
          <table class="lunar">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Horizon</th>
                <th>Phase</th>
                <th class="n">Days</th>
                <th class="n">Effect, bp</th>
                <th class="n">95% interval, bp</th>
                <th class="n">p</th>
                <th class="n">p, BH</th>
                <th class="n">MDE, bp</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {lunar.map((r: any, i: number) => (
                <tr class={i > 0 && lunar[i - 1].series !== r.series ? 'group' : undefined}>
                  <td class="muted">{HORIZON[r.series] ?? r.series}</td>
                  <td>{r.bucket.replace(/_/g, ' ')}</td>
                  <td class="n">{r.n_events?.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
                  <td class="n">{bp(r.effect)}</td>
                  <td class="n muted">{bp(r.ci_low)} to {bp(r.ci_high)}</td>
                  <td class={`n ${r.p_value < 0.05 ? 'ok' : ''}`}>{pv(r.p_value)}</td>
                  <td class="n">{pv(r.p_bh)}</td>
                  <td class="n muted">{bp(r.mde)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          Effect is the mean return inside the phase minus the mean outside it.
          MDE is the smallest effect each test could have detected at 80% power
          with the days that exist. {lunarRawSig === 0
            ? 'No lunar test reaches 5% even before correction.'
            : `${lunarRawSig} of ${lunar.length} reach 5% before correction, about what ${lunar.length} tests produce by chance alone, and ${lunarBonf === 0 ? 'none survives' : `${lunarBonf} survive`} correction.`}
        </p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>
        The study has not been run yet. Once it has, this section carries
        returns bucketed by the eight phases, the effect for each against its
        complement, a bootstrap interval, and the p-value before and after
        correction across the full family.
      </p>
    )}

    <p class="closing">
      If a result does appear above the corrected threshold, read
      <a href="/studies/#bangladesh">the Bangladesh line</a> before believing it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .moon-block { padding-top: 0; padding-left: 0; padding-right: 0; }
  .moon-block :global(.moon-controls) { padding-left: 10%; padding-right: 10%; }

  .readout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(12rem, 1fr));
    gap: 0 2rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
  }
  .readout > div {
    padding: 0.8rem 0 0.9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .readout dt { font-size: var(--label); color: var(--type-muted); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
  .readout dd { margin: 0; font-size: 1.4rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .readout dd.stamp { font-size: 1.05rem; }

  .lunar { margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .lunar td:nth-child(2) { text-transform: capitalize; }
  .lunar tr.group td { border-top: 1px solid var(--type); }
</style>
